WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.086 --> 00:00:02.486
Hi, and welcome.

2
00:00:02.486 --> 00:00:07.362
I'm Laura Hemmingsen, Chief of Staff at
Kestrel Falls Community Hospital,

3
00:00:07.362 --> 00:00:08.650
and starting today, you're my assistant.

4
00:00:08.650 --> 00:00:11.936
Let me tell you a little about us.

5
00:00:11.936 --> 00:00:15.732
We're an independent, not-for-profit community
hospital in Kestrel Falls, Iowa.

6
00:00:15.732 --> 00:00:20.597
About three hundred beds, ten outpatient
clinics, and our own ambulance service.

7
00:00:20.597 --> 00:00:25.261
We have an operating budget of about three
hundred seventy-five million dollars a

8
00:00:25.261 --> 00:00:29.631
year, and our people care for patients
from all over the region.

9
00:00:29.631 --> 00:00:32.564
My job is to help our CEO and our leaders
turn decisions into action.

10
00:00:32.564 --> 00:00:34.296
Your job is to help me do that.

11
00:00:34.296 --> 00:00:38.810
You'll research, analyze, write, and sometimes
tell me things I don't want to hear.

12
00:00:38.810 --> 00:00:41.005
That last part matters most.

13
00:00:41.005 --> 00:00:44.515
This year is going to be a big
one for technology.

14
00:00:44.515 --> 00:00:48.499
You'll hear a lot about our
electronic health record,

15
00:00:48.499 --> 00:00:51.075
and about the other systems
that run this place.

16
00:00:51.075 --> 00:00:53.479
You don't need to be an IT expert.

17
00:00:53.479 --> 00:00:57.793
You need to understand what those
systems do, who depends on them,

18
00:00:57.793 --> 00:01:00.933
and what happens to patients and
staff when they don't work.

19
00:01:00.933 --> 00:01:02.965
So that's your first assignment.

20
00:01:02.965 --> 00:01:07.343
Doug Thielen, our CIO, sent me a
list of our main systems.

21
00:01:07.343 --> 00:01:10.767
I'd like you to map them: what each
one does for the hospital,

22
00:01:10.767 --> 00:01:13.257
and what happens to us if it
goes down for a day.

23
00:01:13.257 --> 00:01:17.719
Then tell me which three we'd most need to
protect during a big change, and why.

24
00:01:17.719 --> 00:01:20.420
You'll use Copilot to help
you work faster.

25
00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:21.847
I'm a believer in it.

26
00:01:21.847 --> 00:01:25.895
But Copilot can be confidently wrong,
so check what it tells you.

27
00:01:25.895 --> 00:01:29.574
I would rather have your honest
reasoning than a polished guess.

28
00:01:29.574 --> 00:01:32.084
And one habit to start on day one.

29
00:01:32.084 --> 00:01:39.093
For every system, ask: who depends on this, and
what breaks for them if it stops?

30
00:01:39.093 --> 00:01:40.160
I'm glad you're here.

31
00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:41.080
Let's get to work.

